#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167000 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6b8daa4-a421-5ee6-82b9-9eb043ad7cf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x) = 2\\sqrt{x^{2} + 12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163419} {:answer/id #uuid "ca01366f-9c8a-51ce-8f15-bed65200a620" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x) = 2\\sqrt{x^{2} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163420} {:answer/id #uuid "cbe873e5-d7ad-55ae-91e9-d36486402d46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x) = 2x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163421} {:answer/id #uuid "13d41fac-776e-5c91-a0ac-84fdd7cd9133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x) = \\frac{8}{5}\\sqrt{x^{2} + 21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163422} {:answer/id #uuid "20b8760e-1b1a-5405-bed3-21cdbc66936b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x) = x^{2} + 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6b8daa4-a421-5ee6-82b9-9eb043ad7cf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x) = 2\\sqrt{x^{2} + 12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163419} :answer-field/id #uuid "4eed9cef-29c7-5b9e-b2e1-4fece31a3301" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cfc6326b-3536-5d27-a880-335a706849db" :question/math-academy-id "q-107999" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $4x\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $2\\,m$ when its velocity is $8\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/017463f3ac8abac9c2b00c4d9a715a4109a5f4bc1b070e0a12c4d3a7bac64351.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $4x\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 4x\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=2\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=8\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8)^{2} & =2(2)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =32-8 \\\\ C & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+24 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4{x}^{2}+48 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4({x}^{2}+12) \\\\ v & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+12}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7992ead3-e5f7-547e-9efc-270d787d50bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{t}{1-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314111} {:answer/id #uuid "29133973-97fb-5199-9500-d43e5e83d14b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2t-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314112} {:answer/id #uuid "8efdf446-ae54-51a7-a71e-2615d299ff1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{4t}{1-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314113} {:answer/id #uuid "8a278244-4267-5d36-9aca-7ad4a24407b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314114} {:answer/id #uuid "46993d2a-bae4-5320-96e0-15424b25d131" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=t-4{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8efdf446-ae54-51a7-a71e-2615d299ff1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{4t}{1-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314113} :answer-field/id #uuid "eda73ff6-3a44-5506-bf98-66be2ece3c2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e97459ef-d10a-5b95-843c-7ee0d441d495" :question/math-academy-id "q-122344" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ its acceleration is $2(x+2)^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=4\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t$ where $t\\in [0,\\frac{1}{4}].$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/ba9988a12d1c70c43a6a010c3578850fe79a9bca01309884fe981b3f7cca512c.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $2(x+2)^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2(x+2)^{3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2(x+2)^{3}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2(x+2)^{3}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)^{4}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)^{4}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+2)^{4}+K \\\\ 8 & =8+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)^{4} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =(x+2)^{4} \\\\ v & =\\pm (x+2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(x+2)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x+2} & =t+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+2} & =-t-C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=-\\frac{1}{2},$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x+2}=-t+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+2} & =-t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+2} & =\\frac{1-2t}{2} \\\\ x+2 & =\\frac{2}{1-2t} \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{1-2t}-2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4t}{1-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314123 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8582586-2993-58ca-894d-f1b2ef69db0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.7\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314125} {:answer/id #uuid "f94514ab-0f3c-54c7-9a2c-d737fdd51916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.1\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314126} {:answer/id #uuid "9bc76b21-dc60-5a6e-92c7-eb9926209590" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.8\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314127} {:answer/id #uuid "f8f35de8-12c0-5363-aa34-016ba8b66bee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.9\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314128} {:answer/id #uuid "abff615b-88fa-5e83-979e-bf362569b025" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.3\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f94514ab-0f3c-54c7-9a2c-d737fdd51916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.1\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314126} :answer-field/id #uuid "18e0157c-d850-5e2a-a393-20dde143490c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "468eb29a-5370-5275-b5a4-4d18d8cf18e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-122347" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of decreasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is ${3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $v=-2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/3c5450b18c5dc58b082faadf4086b6a21c0c9b55afb016090147609a2ccfe719.png)\n\nSince the magnitude of the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int [-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3]\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={3}^{1-x}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=-2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(-2\\sqrt{2})^{2} & ={3}^{1-2}+C \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{3}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3}) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=-\\sqrt{2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3})}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =-\\sqrt{2({3}^{1-1}+\\frac{11}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{28}{3}} \\\\  & ≈-3.1\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}]}
